    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on an embedded C++ project for an automotive ECU and I've hit a wall. We're seeing a slow memory leak that only manifests after about 4-6 hours of continuous operation. It's not crashing the system immediately, but the heap usage just keeps climbing until it hits the limit.\n\nHere is a simplified version of the telemetry loop where I suspect the issue might be:\n\n```cpp\nvoid telemetry_task() {\n    while (system_running) {\n        SensorData* data = new SensorData();\n        data->timestamp = get_timestamp();\n        data->reading = read_sensor();\n\n        if (data->reading > THRESHOLD) {\n            LogEntry* entry = new LogEntry(data);\n            event_queue.push(entry);\n        }\n        \n        // potential issue area\n        process_buffer(data); \n        \n        sleep_ms(10);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nI've checked basic pointer assignments, but I can't find where the allocation isn't being freed. Can you give me a systematic approach to locating the specific leak in this kind of RTOS environment?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A slow leak in an embedded system like an ECU is often a subtle, persistent failure to clean up resources, much like a small, unnoticed trickle of water in a vast system. You need to track the *entire* lifecycle of every allocated object, not just the obvious ones. My first suggestion is to instrument your memory allocation routines—or, if you can't modify `new`/`delete`, wrap them with logging that tracks the allocation address and the call stack at the time of allocation.\n\nLook closely at `process_buffer(data)` and the `LogEntry` creation. If `process_buffer` is internally allocating memory (perhaps for temporary buffers or internal structures) and failing to free them, that's a prime suspect. You must ensure that every `new` has a corresponding `delete`, or better yet, switch to RAII (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization) using smart pointers if your compiler/environment allows it. For a high-reliability ECU, raw `new`/`delete` is often a recipe for disaster, making a memory leak more likely than a well-managed stack object.",
      "source": "target",
